    "date_str": "2026-06-11",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-06-11",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23716_english_2026-06-11.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-06-11-2026-06-11-0063",
    "chunk_index": 63,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "c5034bd6e857f911f2e991c3912360ab",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1389,
    "chunk_text": "Many countries tackling the issue of laws' delays have resorted to alternatives to avoid protracted and costly litigation. The search for alternatives began many years\n\n485 486\n\n[\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 (\u0dc0\u0dc0\u0daf\u0dba) \u0db1\u0dd2\u0db1\u0db1\u0dca\u0daf \u0da2\u0db8\u0dd2\u0dc3\u0dca\u0dc3 \u0db8 \u0dba\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dca\u200d\u0dcf]\n\n2026 \u0da2\u0dd6\u0db1\u0dd2 11\n\nWe already have two statutes which provide for community level dispute resolution using mediation. Those are, the Mediation Boards Act, No. 72 of 1988, as amended, which provides for community level minor dispute resolution and the Mediation (Special Categories of Disputes) Act, No. 21 of 2003, which provides for the establishment of panels for the settlement of identified categories of disputes in identified areas of the country. Those statutes are implemented by the State. There is, however, no subject-specific statute to govern the use of mediation for higher value civil and commercial dispute resolution. Therefore, it is that lacuna that this Bill is trying to fill.\n\nThis Bill incorporates globally accepted features that are intrinsic to the practice of facilitative mediation. The use of mediation guided by standards set out in this Bill will have a positive impact on the dispute resolution ecosystem in this country and will also help ease the case overload in courts. To understand the provisions in the Bill, Sir, it is important to first understand the key features of mediation."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-06-11-2026-06-11",
    "num": "2026-06-11",
    "date_str": "2026-06-11",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-06-11",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23716_english_2026-06-11.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-06-11-2026-06-11-0064",
    "chunk_index": 64,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "158d32c373c1696a905d86a682703c2f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1865,
    "chunk_text": "This Bill incorporates globally accepted features that are intrinsic to the practice of facilitative mediation. The use of mediation guided by standards set out in this Bill will have a positive impact on the dispute resolution ecosystem in this country and will also help ease the case overload in courts. To understand the provisions in the Bill, Sir, it is important to first understand the key features of mediation.\n\nThe mediator is independent and neutral and plays an important role during the mediation process as a communicator, a manager and a facilitator. The mediator, who is trained in the techniques and skills of mediation and who could be from any profession, plays a non-coercive role and has no authority to impose a decision on the disputants.\n\nThe focus is not on the right and wrong, like in litigation. There are no legal arguments to be submitted by lawyers, but disputants have the freedom to discuss all of their issues and concerns and to have them addressed. So, it is a totally non-adversarial process which provides the space to disputants to find creative solutions that are not confined to what is possible under applicable laws. Lawyers and other professionals can be present to assist the parties reach a settlement. The role of the lawyer is\n\ndifferent to their role in a court of law, where everything is decided according to law. A settlement is possible only if the disputants agree to terms that are acceptable to them. Parties have complete control over the outcome.\n\nArbitration is also an Alternative Dispute Resolution - ADR - mechanism. In Sri Lanka, the Arbitration Act of 1995 governs arbitrations.\n\nSir, mediation has proved to have many benefits: it is cost effective and time efficient; it is confidential; it helps improve strained relationships and affords space for creative solutions that are not confined by law."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-06-11-2026-06-11",
    "num": "2026-06-11",
    "date_str": "2026-06-11",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-06-11",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23716_english_2026-06-11.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-06-11-2026-06-11-0065",
    "chunk_index": 65,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "841e1745d9a206b25f585f71a1cf38e4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1835,
    "chunk_text": "Sir, mediation has proved to have many benefits: it is cost effective and time efficient; it is confidential; it helps improve strained relationships and affords space for creative solutions that are not confined by law.\n\nSir, if we take why mediation is important for Sri Lanka, there are several benefits of mediation. The option of resolving disputes through mediation serves to divert cases from courts and assists in reducing the case overload. Sri Lanka, like many countries across the globe, has a serious problem of laws' delays. As at end of June, 2025, there were over 1.13 million cases pending in courts. So, there is a serious need to divert disputes away from courts and to leave the courts to handle disputes that must necessarily be adjudicated within the adversarial process, based on the principles of law. Then, the use of mediation can result in savings for the State in the administration of justice. That is a benefit referred to in the Preamble to the UN Mediation Convention of 2018.\n\nSir, efficient dispute resolution also has a positive impact on the economy. Litigation delays have an adverse impact on trade, business and commerce, which, in turn, impacts on economic progress. That is a negative feature which is often not acknowledged. The need for smarter dispute resolution for the business sector devoid of expense, delay and unpredictability is important for economic development and to attract investors. The business and commercial community must have access to smart conflict resolution options that are time-efficient and cost-effective. Productivity is obviously enhanced due to these benefits with a significant impact on the economy. Businesses, whether large corporates or small enterprises, should be able to focus on their core objectives without being detracted by long-drawn legal battles."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
