    "description": "Hansard of 2026-06-11",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23716_english_2026-06-11.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-06-11-2026-06-11-0068",
    "chunk_index": 68,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "b863d9f28fd44dfae96842d609b72cdc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1856,
    "chunk_text": "Sir, reference of disputes to mediation by courts is provided for in the Bill and it is a very common feature in many jurisdictions around the world. The concept of a mediation clause including the pre-dispute mediation clause is recognized by it. That is the same as the arbitration clause in arbitrations. Disputants can voluntarily agree to mediate even without a pre-dispute mediation clause. Where there is a mediation clause or where a mediation has commenced voluntarily, no action can be filed in court unless mediation has failed. Prescription ceases to run when mediation is resorted to until closure.\n\nThe mediation is brought to a close either by settlement agreed to by the disputants or when no settlement is possible. Where a settlement is reached, a Mediated Settlement Agreement is entered into and signed by the disputants. Such an Agreement is valid and binding as between the parties and is enforceable. Although valid and legally binding, a party can also apply to court to obtain a decree based on the settlement agreement, using the petition and an affidavit procedure. The grant of a decree can be objected to, only on specific grounds set out in the Act. Where a settlement is not reached, a certificate of non-settlement is issued. Importantly, as mentioned previously, unlike in the two community mediation statutes, this Bill does not establish any new entities or panels or boards and does not provide for mandatory reference to mediation.\n\nNow, Sir, let us look at what the situation is globally. There have been some global developments. Globally, mediation is increasingly used for the resolution of many categories of disputes including commercial disputes, intellectual property disputes, family disputes and investor-State disputes, and many countries have enacted mediation legislation because of the benefits it offers."
  },
